# Prospective Notes notebook trash slice

This isolated candidate is not installed in production. The production baseline, its prior 22-check serialization receipt, current notebook receipt and selected requirements bytes are retained exactly. This stage implements a finite source-assisted notebook lifecycle subset; full notebook/product parity, security, superiority and performance gates remain false. No timing measurements were made.

## Implemented behavior

- `notebook-trash` requires `confirmed: true`, a real local notebook ID and the current organization revision. It trashes the selected notebook and every descendant notebook, and active regular notes in that subtree. Already-trashed descendant notebooks receive fresh trash/system/user times. Already-trashed notes retain their previous times. Conflict notes are excluded, as in the released `Folder.notes` selector.
- `notebook-restore` requires confirmation, an organization revision and a trashed local notebook. It restores deleted descendants and regular notes directly in each restored notebook, **including notes trashed separately before the notebook**. Active child notebooks are not themselves processed; their notes remain untouched. A selected notebook whose stored parent is trashed becomes top level, without restoring that parent. Descendant stable IDs remain attached to their restored parents.
- Direct note restore also falls back to Unfiled when its parent is trashed. Direct note trash and restore preserve user timestamps, while updating system/trash times. A direct conflict-note restore retains its conflict properties. Missing-parent imports remain rejected by the existing valid-tree/JEX contract, so the original missing-parent recovery branch is not an accepted stage input.
- The virtual Trash ID is a display projection, never a persisted folder. Active notebook selection for editing/moving remains restricted; the management selector permits local trashed notebooks for restoration. Confirmation text explicitly explains descendant and independently trashed-note behavior.
- Cascades preserve note bodies, tag relationships, native IDs, supported raw metadata, attachment references and binary bytes. Changed notes gain one local revision and a retained previous version; changed display paths also invalidate stale note edits. One organization revision is added per committed operation. These local revisions/history differ from original Joplin revision/sync behavior.
- Each mutation runs through the existing exclusive queue, loads a private state clone, checks the optimistic organization revision, produces another isolated candidate, validates it and calls the existing atomic state-record writer once. A failed commit does not publish a partial cascade. Attachments are neither written nor deleted by trash/restore. This is process-local serialization and atomic record replacement, not a distributed transaction or power-loss durability guarantee.

The original helper performs several ordered per-item writes and can revisit descendants. The candidate applies each final row once. With the fixed clock and valid unshared tree fixtures the final native metadata matches; this does not claim identical write order, per-item event dispatch, clock variation or original database atomicity.

## Bounds and open scope

Existing limits remain: 8 MiB JEX, 4 MiB binary resources, 1,000 notes for this lifecycle subset, 2,000 raw records, 3,000 TAR entries, notebook depth 64, notebook path 200 characters and a 16 MiB serialized state record. The lifecycle primitive is for trusted, already-bounded app states, not arbitrary unbounded caller objects. It creates state clones and history entries; these record/output bounds are not process-memory isolation. No cooperative time/abort budget, multi-process locking or blocked-I/O preemption is added.

Shared notebooks/notes and the reserved conflict notebook refuse before persistence. Conflict notes inside ordinary notebooks are retained and excluded from notebook cascades. This stage does not implement permanent notebook deletion, empty trash, automatic age-based purge, optional `targetFolderId`/`useRestoreFolder`/`toTrashParentId`, synchronization, permissions/read-only policies, original trash command selection logic, resource cleanup, original desktop/database execution or GUI parity. Existing Markdown/search/import/export and all other Notes gaps remain open. JEX excludes local saved-version history. Default JEX export refuses a surviving note's link to an omitted trashed note; export with trash for that complete retained-link subset.

## Original evidence and licensing

Released **Joplin Desktop 3.6.14**, tag `v3.6.14`, commit `7e2765a3857754d12dbadea442b60ceaf9d4b23d`, is bound by retained official tag, release and package metadata. [SOURCE-CONTRACTS.md](SOURCE-CONTRACTS.md), [prior provenance](prior-source-provenance.json), [new provenance](trash-source-provenance.json) and [license/reserved-ID provenance](license-and-special-id-provenance.json) identify exact acquired bytes. Original AGPL-3.0-or-later notices and source bytes remain retained. Candidate code is newly written, source-assisted work; no clean-room or MIT claim is made. Exact original slices run only in test tooling, never as an application wrapper.

The new finite comparison executes nine exact released slices through owned in-memory SQL/load/save adapters and a fixed clock. The final run has **seven top-level trash/restore calls**, **six display-parent calls**, **73 matched final item projections** (five fields each; 365 scalar assertions) and eight oracle contexts. These are separate from the prior notebook study's 26 exact comparisons and two original-only move assertions, and from the historical 22 serialization checks. The released full application, original database, sync engine and GUI execute zero times. The actual candidate HTTP host runs independently; two DOM groups use the real staged client and server through owned transport adapters, not a browser.

## Reproduction and failures

From this stage, run `node run-focused.mjs <unique-run-name>`; Node 24.16.0 was used. Runtime tests write observations only to stage `.local/observations` and owned temporary directories. The collector preserves source snapshots and raw output under a new run directory. No production/results files are written.

Failures remain immutable:

1. `failures/acquisition-v1`: an already-retained BaseModel file caused exclusive-create EEXIST. The next acquisition authenticated identical bytes instead of overwriting.
2. `failures/adaptation-v1`: a source-helper parent directory was missing. The adapter now creates that owned directory. Both acquisition/adapter diagnostics are explicitly tool-console transcriptions, not fabricated raw process logs.
3. `runs/initial-core`: 3/9 passed; six oracle setup failures used the wrong provenance property. Its first collector reported zero counts because the default human reporter was not parsed. Exact raw output and source snapshots remain unchanged; later collector explicitly uses TAP. This was not a behavior comparison pass.
4. `runs/core-v2`: 9/9 passed; `runs/full-v3`: 40/40 passed.
5. `runs/full-v4`: 40/41 passed; one new synthetic export fixture included an unsupported folder `application_data` field. The correction removed only that invalid fixture field; runtime JEX field acceptance was not relaxed. Exact failed source and raw logs remain retained.
6. `runs/full-v5`: 41/41 passed before final documentation. The final source-bound run is separately identified by the freeze receipt, rather than rebinding earlier runs to later bytes.

A further preflight-only failure stopped before starting tests because the selected requirements file had changed externally; the old requirements snapshot, original failed script and transcribed console diagnosis remain separate from the authorized current epoch.

The strict preservation manifest covers baseline, original, failures, both requirements epochs, every historical run and final candidate/evidence. Generated `.local` observations are copied by the final collector into the final run and excluded from the frozen artifact set. Earlier raw runs preserve stdout/source snapshots, not later reconstructed detailed observations. Production Notes and the selected shared host/storage/prior receipts are verified against the unchanged original baseline. Requirements changed externally while this stage was paused: `failures/freeze-preflight-v1` preserves the failed preflight and exact script, and `epochs/current` binds separate current bytes before the new final attempt. Old baseline bytes and all prior runs remain untouched. The final attempt verifies the new requirements epoch unchanged before/after, 48 source rows structurally exact and all 68 original capability core definitions unchanged; three notebook evidence/prose fields differ. This does not claim full capability rows or the old requirements file are current-byte identical.
